#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\ny' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-3y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-3L\\{y\\} & =L\\{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/id #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-21861" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180acc58-6cf1-560f-ac90-861b9673af03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180acc58-6cf1-560f-ac90-861b9673af03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315258} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a3d655-9ec1-5074-b9cc-66dad632ea49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4f34a7a-fc84-5a59-9569-b602aa08ac47" :question/math-academy-id "q-342263" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)={e}^{t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{(t+2)-2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L\\{{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5cce2c3-c588-5be6-adc1-bedf079c277a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316231} {:answer/id #uuid "bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-\\pi s}}{(s+1)(s^2+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc993c55-c472-52a8-9bc3-380b2766789d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{-\\pi s}}{(s+1)(s^2+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553242} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6351e9-8beb-53ac-b1c7-523b43971f65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5fbd03e-952d-5ca5-b272-4b87d3810500" :question/math-academy-id "q-342267" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808a3dc9-595d-505a-9f10-17cbb9e73f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553129} :answer-field/id #uuid "237b2bf3-3e95-50eb-9a6d-6377b31e46da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e21a8112-0441-5b7a-9e9e-a86738fe8b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-342542" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}-2y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=4{e}^{t-3}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{4u(t-3)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{(t+3)-3}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L\\{4{e}^{t}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot \\frac{4}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}-2L\\{y\\} & =L\\{4{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-3}\\} \\\\ sY(s)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f96584d-4303-56a1-ab81-33e926b99d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552889} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d6b55c-5888-5098-8079-b16eed82738c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "137d2706-a3fe-517c-a8b7-db4dd05fae28" :question/math-academy-id "q-342547" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+4}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-2)=sY(s)+2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $f(t)=sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{sin\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)sin\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s+4}+\\frac{3{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{se^{-\\pi s/3}}{(s^2+4)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c87e25c-189d-5204-85e6-f4884b92e797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{se^{-\\pi s/3}}{(s^2+4)(s+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553067} :answer-field/id #uuid "45ab7f5f-7155-55c1-b6f0-ecb774ed5d33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da40e310-ec0b-5b91-ae08-d59c190a3606" :question/math-academy-id "q-342771" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y={u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /3}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}+4y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L\\{{y}^{′}\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL\\{{y}^{′}\\}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $f(t)=cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /3)s}\\cdot L\\{cos\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/3}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{y}^{′}\\}+4L\\{y\\} & =L\\{{u}_{\\pi /3}(t)cos\\,(2t-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})\\} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s{e}^{-\\pi s/3}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009076e7-87b7-576e-bbb7-5638940d7dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} {:answer/id #uuid "032231c3-dc3e-5b5e-8890-15f57b1864de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315134} {:answer/id #uuid "b5480eaa-19d8-5c53-8739-e8b4885f637b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{10-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316145} {:answer/id #uuid "c700e6db-2a5d-50ae-bb39-9ca51c2cb886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}-\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316243} {:answer/id #uuid "e3afda1a-1ec9-56cc-b1c0-d2d20aec3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009076e7-87b7-576e-bbb7-5638940d7dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} :answer-field/id #uuid "90fe901c-f8c8-565f-93b4-e7e2fe0bf635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d571581-f4a8-5a66-b8eb-3c5c0101beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a492d9-cf7a-517a-a388-35a26f6d769e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{7-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553006} {:answer/id #uuid "58f86b27-aaef-5734-b574-cf851cfc63b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{3t-5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553059} {:answer/id #uuid "5f13f1d2-9669-574a-b992-547d64c416c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}+\\frac{5-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa54b773-38b6-5c26-897c-a183baeabac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{7-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553211} {:answer/id #uuid "bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bacb037c-ce80-5974-9d4b-3e4d832343d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553239} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbf215b-97cc-524e-b504-f280dea50225" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "305f1606-0562-59f2-b1e4-3359528e4a32" :question/math-academy-id "q-342786" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{5}{s-3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s-3)+B(s-3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}-\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s-3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{t-2}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(-\\frac{3t-5}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=5{e}^{3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3(t-2)}-\\frac{3t-5}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553027} {:answer/id #uuid "53bc9662-bdf2-5c5a-b51c-9e4907f321d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553047} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3a6c29-f691-50d0-a36e-fb4594dc00b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553189} {:answer/id #uuid "f6208ede-9d9d-5811-9f98-c396b7811777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553354} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7c7845-1582-524e-ac10-277c2817e843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a57e159-8b9f-5e50-a811-ef9584e66726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553027} :answer-field/id #uuid "22643dcd-e934-597d-ad21-1b1e44a5206d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "847985b8-e342-54d5-a829-10e82cf593d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-342788" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2=A((-1)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,5A=2\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n2=4A+C\\,⟹\\,C=2-\\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{-s+1}{{s}^{2}+4})=\\frac{2}{5}(\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{2}{5}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}({e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term in $Y(s).$ By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}-cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\frac{1}{2}sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-\\pi /2))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}[{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=\\frac{1}{5}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0126ee72-ff17-5f29-8f3a-2ad3ee6df269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552869} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552985} {:answer/id #uuid "46be3b8a-e417-5b1c-bcce-748fd498356f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553013} {:answer/id #uuid "ee49073d-5c8b-5dfd-ba92-d96d34e4671d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-3(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553339} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e26abd-98f0-5b0a-bf6e-57ca430a9667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-3(t-2)}+{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a5bb7a9-1a25-50f0-8b9f-6a548165a532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552985} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c95e702-817d-5000-89eb-1d91beb9276d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "965ae37e-9b0d-55ee-82fd-8e26fc17d04a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342810" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4e0b48-8667-556e-81ec-ea459847a89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552922} {:answer/id #uuid "32046168-bf15-539e-a0bf-0354d06e8d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}+{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552966} {:answer/id #uuid "9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553177} {:answer/id #uuid "c72baafc-5ab2-555e-85d3-8cbb91b8e24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553267} {:answer/id #uuid "d81c41ca-a50b-5956-9a30-f2989bbe8749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{-5t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9703e3d9-bfb7-5f17-8106-1258f1821bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7838ff7-32b3-503c-9d51-a06dd6fe14d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8979fd62-065d-5783-b1c9-4baf45f041fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-342811" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-5(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-4)(s+5)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-4)(s+5)}=\\frac{A}{s-4}+\\frac{B}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+5)+B(s-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(9)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-9)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{1}{s-4}-\\frac{1}{s+5}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{9}({L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+5}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{-5t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}({e}^{4(t-3)}-{e}^{-5(t-3)}).\n$$"}]}
